.TH JOTUN 1 "2026-11-13" "jotun" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
jotun \- hypervisor-hosted sandbox for unmodified Linux binaries
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B jotun
.RI [ -m " manifest" ]...
.RI [ kernel-image ]
.RI [ init-arg ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B jotun
is a small hypervisor that runs unmodified native Linux binaries inside a
freestanding guest kernel (a library OS) with real privilege separation. It
runs over Linux KVM (x86-64 and arm64) and macOS Hypervisor.framework (arm64),
with the guest architecture equal to the host. The intended use is to build and
cross-compile real projects inside the sandbox, using a host toolchain mounted
into the guest over 9P.
.PP
Each guest runs one
.I kernel-image
(the library OS, normally the file
.B libos.elf
produced by the build) and an init program. A guest's configuration comes
from a manifest named with
.BR -m ,
or, when no manifest is given, from the environment and the trailing
command-line arguments.
.PP
With one or more
.B -m
options, each names the manifest of one guest; all guests share the single
.I kernel-image
given as the first bare argument. With no
.BR -m ,
a single guest runs, taking its manifest from
.B JOTUN_MANIFEST
if set, and otherwise running
.I kernel-image
with the trailing
.IR init-arg s
as the init program and its arguments.
.PP
If no
.I kernel-image
is given on the command line,
.B jotun
resolves one in order: the file named by
.BR JOTUN_KERNEL ;
a kernel lifted from a bundle (the object
.BR boot/kernel );
.B libos.elf
next to the
.B jotun
binary (a build tree) or in
.B ../libexec/jotun
relative to it (an install tree); and
.B libos.elf
in the current directory. Failing all of these, the file
.B test_guest.elf
in the current directory is used. An installed
.B jotun
therefore boots from just a manifest, with no kernel path on the command line.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI -m " manifest"
Bring up a guest configured by
.IR manifest .
May be repeated, once per guest, up to the compiled-in guest limit. All guests
named this way share the one
.I kernel-image
argument.
.SH MANIFEST
A manifest is a text file, one directive per line. Blank lines and lines
beginning with
.B #
are ignored. The recognized directives are:
.TP
.BI rootfs " host:path"
Serve the guest root from the live host directory
.I path
(read-only), so a distro root filesystem sits at
.BR / .
The kernel then comes from the command-line image.
.TP
.BI bundle " path"
Serve the guest root from a bundle depot at
.IR path .
.TP
.BI ref " name"
Name the root tree within the bundle (default
.BR root ).
.TP
.BI subtree " path"
Serve only the given subtree of the bundle root.
.TP
.BI mount " name source [ref] [subtree]"
Graft a shared tree at the top-level
.I name
in the guest root. A
.I source
of
.BI host: path
serves a live host directory; appending the token
.B rw
makes that host mount writable, so guest writes land on the host. Any other
.I source
is a bundle depot, with an optional
.I ref
and
.IR subtree .
.TP
.BI init " program [arg ...]"
The init program run inside the guest and its arguments.
.TP
.BI name " string"
The guest's name on the inter-guest network.
.TP
.BI cmdline " string"
The kernel command line the guest exposes as
.BR /proc/cmdline .
The whole value is kept verbatim, spaces included.
.TP
.B console interactive
Wire the guest console to the host terminal, giving init an interactive tty.
.TP
.BI net " allow cidr ports [proto]"
Permit outbound traffic to
.I cidr
on
.I ports
(optionally restricted to
.IR proto ).
Without any
.B net
directive the default policy denies outbound traffic.
.TP
.BI origin " url"
.TQ
.BI key " keyspec"
.TQ
.BI label " string"
Pull a signed bundle from
.I origin
before boot, verifying it against the trusted
.IR key .
The guest refuses to boot if the pull cannot be verified.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B JOTUN_KERNEL
Path to the kernel image to load when none is given on the command line. Takes
precedence over the automatic search.
.TP
.B JOTUN_MANIFEST
Manifest for the single guest when no
.B -m
is given.
.TP
.B JOTUN_VCPUS
Number of vcpus per guest (default 1, capped at the compiled-in maximum).
.TP
.B JOTUN_HZ
Preemption timer rate in ticks per second. Preemption of guest user code is
opt-in: with
.B JOTUN_HZ
unset or 0 no timer runs and the scheduler is cooperative.
.TP
.B JOTUN_NO_PREEMPT
When set, disable the in-kernel preemption that is otherwise on by default.
.TP
.B JOTUN_MAX_EXITS
Stop a guest after this many VM exits (0, the default, means no limit). A
debugging aid.
.TP
.B JOTUN_DEBUG
Enable verbose host-side debug logging.
.TP
.B JOTUN_ORIGIN ", " JOTUN_KEY ", " JOTUN_LABEL ", " JOTUN_CACHE ", " JOTUN_BUNDLE ", " JOTUN_REF ", " JOTUN_NAME
Environment fallbacks for the single no-manifest guest, matching the manifest
directives of the same name.
.B JOTUN_CACHE
names the local directory a pulled bundle is cached in.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B libos.elf
The guest library OS kernel, built alongside
.BR jotun .
Normally passed as the
.I kernel-image
argument.
.TP
.B test_guest.elf
Fallback kernel image loaded from the current directory when no image is given
and none is present in a bundle.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run an interactive shell from a Debian root filesystem on the host, with the
kernel source tree mounted writable:
.PP
.RS
.nf
jotun -m build.manifest libos.elf
.fi
.RE
.PP
where
.B build.manifest
contains:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rootfs host:/home/user/debroot
mount ksrc host:/home/user/src rw
console interactive
init bin /bin/sh
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B jotun
exits 0 on a clean shutdown, and non-zero if a guest fails to come up or a
signed pull cannot be verified.
.SH SEE ALSO
The project README and the documents under
.BR doc/ ,
in particular
.B doc/sandbox-build.md
(building and running) and
.B doc/unikernel-design.md
(design).
